items.summary:
The escaper considered x.attr, x['key'] and x.method() safe whenever {% import ... as x %} appeared anywhere in the template. Macro calls haven't needed this rule since Twig 3.0. It only matched ordinary variables that have the name of an alias imported in another block or macro, and printed them without escaping:

{% block header %}{% import _self as item %}{{ item.hello() }}{% endblock %}
{% block content %}{% for item in items %}{{ item.body }}{% endfor %}{% endblock %}

Here, item.body is printed raw. This leads to XSS when the value comes from an attacker. The same happens with an import in a macro and an argument of the same name in another macro. It also happens with a template-level import placed after the block that uses the name.

The rule is removed. Macro calls through an alias are still considered safe.

